{:edb/db-before-t 2680 :edb/db-after-t 2681 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535993 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:28:53.334Z" 13194139535993 true] [17592186124406 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378612 13194139535993 true] [17592186124406 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378619 13194139535993 true] [17592186124511 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378598 13194139535993 true] [17592186124511 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378605 13194139535993 true] [17592186125072 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535993 true] [17592186125072 1100 "Consider the vector space $C[0, 1]$ of all continuous functions $f: [0, 1] → R$ and its subset $K$ containing all constant functions.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $K$ is closed under function addition\n2. $K$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $K$ is a subspace of $C[0, 1]$\n4. $K$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0, 1]$\n\n*Hint: $f$ is a constant function if $f(x) = const$ for all $x ∈ [0, 1]$.*" 13194139535993 false] [17592186125072 1100 "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $K$ containing all constant functions.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $K$ is closed under function addition\n- $K$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $K$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $K$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\n*Hint: $f$ is a constant function if $f(x)=\\text{const}$ for all $x\\in [0,1].$*" 13194139535993 true] [17592186125072 1145 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The sum of two constant functions is a constant function. So, $K$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. The product of a constant function by a scalar is again a constant function. So, $K$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $K$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378598 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378599 13194139535993 true] [17592186378598 1089 "q-43776" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378598 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535993 true] [17592186378598 1100 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $L$ containing all invertible $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378598 1113 #uuid "805bbacf-53a7-54d0-91ca-ce2a2a5f406a" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378598 1145 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $L.$ So, $L$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example if $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=0,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $L.$ So, $L$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $L$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378600 13194139535993 true] [17592186378599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378601 13194139535993 true] [17592186378599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378602 13194139535993 true] [17592186378599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378603 13194139535993 true] [17592186378599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378604 13194139535993 true] [17592186378599 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535993 true] [17592186378599 1041 "selection" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378599 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378604 13194139535993 true] [17592186378599 1110 #uuid "572ecd56-bc1b-5250-8455-9cb9aa201e0d" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378600 1034 "I and IV only" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378600 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535993 true] [17592186378600 1111 #uuid "47d40f50-bc19-5575-9aad-fc5fc6b8b5e2" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378601 1034 "II and IV only" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378601 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535993 true] [17592186378601 1111 #uuid "8dcea519-dd0d-5426-91be-2b40b969c072" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378602 1034 "I only" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378602 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535993 true] [17592186378602 1111 #uuid "f62c2052-e2a0-535c-86c8-f5709e2f8f70" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378603 1034 "I, II, and III only" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378603 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535993 true] [17592186378603 1111 #uuid "fc66abf8-c4ea-505f-a330-a6435fc686f0" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378604 1034 "IV only" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378604 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535993 true] [17592186378604 1111 #uuid "62f5145f-6879-5927-91db-9243b2eb949f" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378605 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378606 13194139535993 true] [17592186378605 1089 "q-94625" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378605 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535993 true] [17592186378605 1100 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $B={[\\begin{aligned}0 & a \\\\ 1-a & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a\\in R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378605 1113 #uuid "98a80204-c0e6-5c6a-a797-0dc8baa9580c" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378605 1145 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}={A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =2[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378607 13194139535993 true] [17592186378606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378608 13194139535993 true] [17592186378606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378609 13194139535993 true] [17592186378606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378610 13194139535993 true] [17592186378606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378611 13194139535993 true] [17592186378606 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535993 true] [17592186378606 1041 "selection" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378606 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378611 13194139535993 true] [17592186378606 1110 #uuid "3c2241f4-3519-5c9e-a609-17c9cde364e2" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378607 1034 "I and IV only" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378607 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535993 true] [17592186378607 1111 #uuid "0283212e-6a0e-589b-93a3-f2e42e1ba2cf" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378608 1034 "II and IV only" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378608 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535993 true] [17592186378608 1111 #uuid "a1cf6c35-3bb0-5766-9d03-acdc85b0bf2d" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378609 1034 "I only" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378609 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535993 true] [17592186378609 1111 #uuid "efe69645-37b1-5bf5-acb2-a01df4ba0aa6" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378610 1034 "I, II, and III only" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378610 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535993 true] [17592186378610 1111 #uuid "fd8d172d-c4bc-59c1-bccd-24c6192838b1" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378611 1034 "IV only" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378611 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535993 true] [17592186378611 1111 #uuid "4d776bab-d4cb-5539-990f-c4b7c9ea5d09" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378612 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378613 13194139535993 true] [17592186378612 1089 "q-94632" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378612 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535993 true] [17592186378612 1100 "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $L$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $1.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ is closed under function addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378612 1113 #uuid "7ad169bd-a8be-5b32-a6b6-b79de58801b4" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378612 1145 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=1-x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)+g(x) & =x+(1-x) \\\\  & =(x-x)+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)=x$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot x=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $L$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $L$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378613 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378614 13194139535993 true] [17592186378613 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378615 13194139535993 true] [17592186378613 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378616 13194139535993 true] [17592186378613 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378617 13194139535993 true] [17592186378613 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378618 13194139535993 true] [17592186378613 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535993 true] [17592186378613 1041 "selection" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378613 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378618 13194139535993 true] [17592186378613 1110 #uuid "5b82e59a-6ab5-5153-be4e-52c0d46ac026" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378614 1034 "I and IV only" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378614 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535993 true] [17592186378614 1111 #uuid "ef0db8c2-ab95-532f-afcd-3f4028bf09f4" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378615 1034 "II and IV only" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378615 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535993 true] [17592186378615 1111 #uuid "ea6d481f-1780-55a1-8d9b-84801d715e59" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378616 1034 "I only" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378616 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535993 true] [17592186378616 1111 #uuid "7d4b00e6-793e-5623-afe3-47e43a6b4b06" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378617 1034 "I, II, and III only" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378617 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535993 true] [17592186378617 1111 #uuid "890afd52-6899-5adf-8229-e279ea7ff62b" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378618 1034 "IV only" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378618 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535993 true] [17592186378618 1111 #uuid "f5873600-8b60-5a63-812d-3f5cd47fa2ec" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378619 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378620 13194139535993 true] [17592186378619 1089 "q-94635" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378619 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535993 true] [17592186378619 1100 "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $C$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $C$ is closed under function addition\n- $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $C$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $C$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378619 1113 #uuid "9702aa0e-9262-5db2-baf6-0c28b2c26439" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378619 1145 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $g(x)=1-{x}^{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)+g(x) & ={x}^{3}+(1-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{3}-{x}^{3})+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $C$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{3}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $C$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $C$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139535993 true] [17592186378620 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378621 13194139535993 true] [17592186378620 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378622 13194139535993 true] [17592186378620 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378623 13194